# Line endings, pinned where they are load-bearing.
#
# Git stores every text file with LF. What lands in a working tree depends on
# the machine: Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so a checkout
# there materializes CRLF. That is normally harmless, and this repository ran
# for weeks without noticing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the *.sql rule below is not cosmetic
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sqlx embeds each migration at compile time and records a SHA-384 of the file
# *bytes* in the _sqlx_migrations table. On every later start it re-hashes the
# embedded migration and compares. A mismatch is a hard failure:
#
#     migration error: migration 1 was previously applied but has been modified
#
# which surfaces as a panic during Tauri's setup hook — the app exits with code
# 101 and no window ever appears.
#
# CRLF changes those bytes. 0001_core.sql is 1577 bytes with LF and 1635 with
# CRLF, and the two hash to completely different values. So without this rule
# the checksum depends on the git configuration of whichever machine compiled
# the binary, and a database created by an LF build is unopenable by a CRLF
# build and vice versa. This is not hypothetical: it is exactly how the v0.1.0
# release binary, built on a Windows runner, came to reject a database created
# by a local build.
#
# The dangerous property is that it is silent until it is catastrophic. Adding
# a .gitattributes *later*, once releases are in users' hands, flips every
# migration checksum at once and hard-crashes every existing installation with
# no in-app recovery. That is why this file exists now, while the only affected
# database was the author's own.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Generated by ts-rs, which writes "\n" literals as raw bytes on every platform.
# The CI drift check already compensates with --ignore-cr-at-eol, but pinning
# them means it no longer has to.
src/shared/ipc/bindings/*.ts text eol=lf

# Shell scripts are executed by Git Bash on Windows runners; CR bytes at the end
# of a line become part of the command and fail in ways that read as nonsense.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.woff2 binary
*.msi binary
*.exe binary
*.sig binary
